firefox: pin the accent block of the content sheet in its check

The content sheet gains one block of the about: and chrome:// addresses
that pins nine accent names of the in-content pages. The check now reads
that block. It pins one scope line, the root head at column 0 and two
close lines. It pins one line per name, in the order of the content
table, and allows no custom property outside the block. Each hex name
must hold the chrome root value of its role. Each link pair must read at
4.5:1 or more on the ground of its half. Seven new control copies bite:
- no scope, the shape that leaks into web pages;
- a web prefix in the scope line;
- the nested layout;
- a name outside the set;
- a value that differs from the chrome sheet;
- a link half under the floor;
- a missing name.

The check fails the writer of today, because the sheet holds 4 lines
and not 18. The writer change follows in its own commit.

firefox: read line 2 of each owned sheet in assert-ff-profiles.sh

The content sheet gains a root rule, so a search for ":root {" no
longer tells the chrome sheet from the content sheet. The check reads
line 2 of each owned sheet: ":root {" for the chrome sheet and "* {"
for the content sheet. A new control swaps the two bodies of a written
profile, and the check must reject it.

firefox: read the content sheets against the map in assert-ff-agree.sh

The map gains content rows under the prefix "content:", one per
palette source of the content sheet. The check bakes the two content
sheets beside the chrome sheets and compares the build products with a
fresh run. It then reads every content name against the chrome value
of the same role in each half. The build writes content-light.css and
content-dark.css and passes them to the check. Two control copies bite:
a content value that differs from the chrome value, and a content name
that the sheet does not set.

The check fails the writer of today, because its map holds no content
row. The writer change follows in its own commit.

firefox: pin the in-content accent of Firefox 157 to the highlight

Firefox 157 paints the accent of about:preferences and its sibling
pages from a violet of tokens-shared.css under the Nova preference. The
add-on arm and the native theme arm show the same violet. The content
sheet of the writer is a user sheet, and a user declaration under
!important wins over every author declaration. The sheet therefore
gains one block under url-prefix("about:") and url-prefix("chrome://").
The block pins nine accent names of the in-content pages: the primary,
hover, active and selected accents, two text names and three link
names. The chrome:// scope reaches the Settings subdialogs, which are
chrome documents in a content frame. A web page never has either
address.

A content table in ffmap.cpp binds the six hex names to the highlight,
highlightText, highlightHover and highlightActive rows of the map. One
palette thus feeds both sheets, and the chrome sheet does not change.
The map lists the content rows under the prefix "content:".

The links follow the accent by derivation. A dark highlight reads under
3:1 in the light content half. Each link name therefore takes a
light-dark() pair. Each half is the nearest HCY shade of the highlight
that reads at 4.5:1 plus a margin of 0.05 on the ground of its half. The
shade keeps the hue and the chroma of the highlight. The ground of a
half is the lower of the two grounds that the pages paint under a link.
It is the canvas #f7f6fb for the light half and the box #252428 for the
dark half. The hover and active states move 0.03 and 0.06 of luma
further from the ground. The margin keeps a value over the floor in the
check, which computes the ratio in other arithmetic. The helpers live in
the Firefox writer, so the GTK writer binary does not change.

firefox: pin the content names and check them in the build

The pin gains the five names that the content sheet alone sets, and
its header names both generated sheets. The script mkfloors.sh takes
the name of the CMake variable as its second argument. The build holds
the floor of the content runs from it. The name check runs over
content-light.css and content-dark.css with that floor, beside the two
chrome runs.

firefox: pin the promo of the app menu to the panel colours

Firefox 157 paints the promo of the Help subview of the app menu from
the violet of its design tokens under the Nova preference. The promo
is a shadow host that declares its ground, its border and its text
names on itself. A declaration of the root therefore never reaches
them, and the chrome sheet gains one rule on the host. The rule holds
the ground at the panel colour, the border at the highlight and the
text at the panel text. Each value is a row of the map. The link of
the promo reads the three link names through the host, so the rule
pins them there as well. No other link of the chrome moves.

Each link state is a shade of the highlight by the search of the
content sheet, on the panel colour. The search moves toward the end,
black or white, that reads higher on that colour, and not by the half
of the scheme. A promo table of the map lists the three palette rows
under the prefix "promo:", and the checks of the build read them.

firefox: pin the new tab names of the content sheet in its check

The accent block of the content sheet gains three names of the new tab
page. They are the page ground, the search box ground and the primary
text. The check reads twelve names in the block in place of nine, and
the sheet holds 21 lines in place of 18. Each new name must hold the
chrome root value of its row: the toolbar colour, the field colour and
the toolbar text. One new control copy bites: the new tab text at the
value of the other colour file, which the check must reject.

The check fails the writer of today, because the sheet holds 18 lines
and not 21. The writer change follows in its own commit.

firefox: read the block rows of the map in assert-ff-agree.sh

The map gains rows under seven more prefixes, one per scoped block of
the chrome sheet. The prefixes are ring, bar, tabbutton, account,
trust, tabgroup and private. A block row names a chrome row, as a promo
row does. The check reads every prefixed row against the value of that
chrome row in each chrome sheet. One function thus covers the promo
rows and the block rows. The map must hold one row of each prefix, and
the count of the chrome rows leaves every prefixed row out. Two more
control copies bite: a ring value apart from the highlight, and a trust
name that the sheet does not set.

The check fails the writer of today, because its map holds no ring row.
The writer change follows in its own commit.

firefox: check the scoped blocks of the chrome sheet in the build

The chrome sheet gains seven scoped blocks of colour names. It also
gains the rule of the bar button, the separator token of the navigation
bar and five rules of the sidebar tree row. The new check reads one
chrome sheet and holds seven contracts. Each block stands once and holds
its names in the order of the writer, with one hex colour each. Each
name holds the root value of its chrome row. The bar button holds a one
pixel border in the highlight. The separator token holds none. The four
guard rules of the tree hold the browser values of a selected row. The
hovered row holds the highlight at 0.20 over the sidebar ground, in the
eight bit mix of the renderer. Each root link name reads at 4.5:1 or
more on the panel colour. Twelve control copies bite, at least one per
contract.

The build runs the check over the light sheet and the dark sheet. It
fails the writer of today, because the sheet holds no ring rule. The
writer change follows in its own commit.

firefox: pin the chrome links to the link colour of the scheme

Under an add-on theme, Firefox 157 paints the links of its panels, of
its dialogs and of the address bar view from a fixed cyan. Under the
native theme it paints them from the system link colour. The chrome
sheet gains the three link names at the root, and the two arms agree.
The source is the KDE link colour of the scheme where it reads at 4.5:1
plus the margin of the check on the panel colour. Else the source is
the highlight moved on the panel colour, as the promo rule of the app
menu derives it. One helper gives the root rows and the promo rule
their values. The promo link thus takes the link colour of the scheme
where that colour wins. The hover and active states move 0.03 and 0.06
of luma toward the end that reads higher on the panel colour. The map
lists the three rows.

firefox: pin twelve chrome names and two button rows at the root

Firefox 157 and 158 paint twelve chrome names from their own tokens
where the map holds the matching role. The chrome rows gain them. The
toolbar row gains the page area before the first paint. The field rows
gain the text field ground and text of the chrome documents. The border
row gains the sidebar border, and the focus row gains the field focus
border. The highlight rows gain the tab line and the field selection
pair that the private window theme sets. The button hover and active
rows gain the close button of a tab and the muted button states. Two
rows join the map: the button ground and the button text of the scheme.
They paint the search mode chip of the address field.

firefox: set the chrome blocks that a root pin cannot reach

Seven surfaces of Firefox 157 and 158 declare their colour names on an
element of their own. A declaration of the root therefore never reaches
them, and the chrome sheet gains one scoped rule for each. The current
row of the Open Tabs panel takes the highlight on both ring stops. The
notification bar takes the toolbar text and the highlight icon, and its
button takes a one pixel border in the highlight. The close button of a
tab and the new tab button of the vertical strip take the button hover
and active colours. The account promos of the app menu and of the
account panel, every vibrant promo and the trust panel take the panel
colours. The name field of the tab group editor takes the field pair.
The private browsing panel link holds the root link colour.

A block table of the map lists the rows of each rule under its prefix,
and the checks of the build read them.

firefox: hide the navigation bar line and paint the hovered tree row

Firefox paints a one pixel line above the navigation bar from the
system shadow colour under the native theme, while the static theme
hides it. The chrome sheet gains a token block that holds the separator
style at none, so the two arms agree.

The Bookmarks sidebar paints the tree row under the pointer with a
fixed grey mix. The chrome sheet gains five rules on the places tree of
the sidebar. The hovered row takes the highlight at 0.20 over the
sidebar ground, in the eight bit mix of the renderer. That is the row
that the Qt style paints under the pointer. The other four rules keep
the browser values of a selected row under the pointer. They hold with
and without the focus, under the native theme and under a static theme.

firefox: pin the new tab page ground, box and text to the chrome colours

The new tab page reads three names of the theme consumer for its page
ground, its search box and its primary text. Under the native theme no
key sets them, and the page paints the Nova tokens. The content table
gains three rows. The page ground takes the toolbar colour, the search
box takes the field colour and the text takes the toolbar text. The
accent block of the content sheet holds twelve names, and the map lists
the three content rows.

firefox: pin the names of round A and check them in the build

The pin gains the 26 names that the chrome sheet and the content sheet
now set. Its header names the token files that declare them. It also
names the sheet that reads the two field selection names, which a
script of the theme consumer declares. The build holds the floors of
the name checks from mkfloors.sh: 81 for the chrome sheets and 12 for
the content sheets. The floor of the chrome rows of the map is 25.

firefox: pin the image area and the least version in the manifest check

The manifest of the static theme gains two keys. Each half keeps its
gradient image in the toolbars at the top of the window through
backgrounds_area. The manifest names the first release of Firefox that
takes a gradient image through strict_min_version. The check now reads
both keys. Each half must hold the area top_toolbars, and the manifest
must name the least version 153.0. Six new control copies bite:
- the area key under another name in both halves;
- the area window in both halves;
- no area key in the dark half alone;
- the least version key under another name;
- a star in place of the last number of the version;
- the version of Firefox 140.

The check fails the writer of today, because its manifest names no
least version. The writer change follows in its own commit.

firefox: keep the gradient of the static theme in the top toolbars

The manifest aligns the gradient image of the title bar at the left
top, and it sets no area for the image. Firefox then paints the image
behind the whole window. With vertical tabs the image fills the tab
strip and the sidebar. The chrome sheet of the writer paints the tool
box above them with its own gradient. Two gradients thus meet at the
lower edge of the tool box.

Each half now sets backgrounds_area to top_toolbars. The image then
stays in the toolbars at the top, and the tab strip takes the frame
colour. The sidebar panel keeps the sidebar colour. In a QtCurve
window, too, the title bar alone holds the gradient.

Firefox 153 is the first release whose theme schema takes a gradient
object as a background, and an older release refuses the manifest. The
manifest therefore names 153.0 as its least version. Firefox 156 is the
first release that reads the area key. The theme schema of Firefox 153
to 155 takes the key as a plain string, so those releases load the
manifest too. The writer adds both keys only when a half carries the
gradient image, so a flat title bar still gives the manifest of today.

The doc comment of the new area constant states where the image
paints. The doc comment of the tiling now says that a gradient fills
its area, so no tile repeats.

firefox: check the pairs and the blocks of the content sheet

The content sheet gains pairs of the two content halves and blocks on
the hosts that declare their own colour names. It also gains a
placeholder rule and the rules of the blocked site page. The checks of
the build follow these changes.

The content check reads 26 names in the root rule of the accent block
in place of 12. A hex name holds the chrome root value of its role. A
pair name holds that value in the half of the scheme of the colour
file. Each text half reads at 4.5:1 or more on the grounds of its
half: the canvas, the box and the button grounds. Each link half reads
at that floor on the canvas, the box, the information box and the view
hover. Each share of the deemphasized text stands from 69 to 100. The
page text at that share reads at the floor on the view hover. Eighteen
control copies bite.

A new check reads the 14 content blocks, the placeholder rule and the
blocked site rules of one content sheet. Each block stands once in the
order of the writer, and each name holds the value of its anchor in the
root rule. A hovered menu row holds the highlight with the highlight
text, or a ground apart from the view hover with the button text. Each
placeholder share stands from 54 to 100. The page text and the field
text at that share read at the floor on the box and on the field of
their half. Fourteen control copies bite.

The map check reads the pair rows and the content block rows against
the content sheets of both colour files. The checks fail the writer of
today, because its content sheet holds no pair and no content block.
The writer changes follow in their own commits.

firefox: pin the button, row and text colours of the about: pages

Under Nova, the about: pages and their dialogs take violet tokens for
the button hover and press, the hovered rows and the page text. The
content sheet gains these names at the root, each as light-dark() of
the two content halves. The half of the scheme takes the role: the
shades of the button ladder, the view hover and the window, field and
button colours. The other half takes the same rule on the canvas and
the box of the browser. A text there keeps the contrast of its role on
its own ground of the scheme. It also reads at 4.5:1 on every ground of
that half.

The disabled primary ground takes the highlight, and the table header
text takes the highlight text. The muted buttons take the button
colours of the chrome. The deemphasized text keeps its mix of the
current colour. Its share is the least share at which the page text and
the button text read at 4.5:1 on each ground of their half. The
placeholder of a field keeps the mix of the user agent sheet. Its least
share lets the page text and the field text read at 4.5:1 on the box
and the field of their half. The links and the information icon read at
that floor on the canvas, the box, the information box and the view
hover. The three new tab names take pairs, so Firefox View paints the
canvas in the light half.

Two roles join the map: the ground and the text of a popup menu item
under the pointer. They take the step of the popup menu ladder that the
style fills, with the button text. Where the settings use the highlight
for menus, they take the highlight pair. The map lists the pair rows
with the content rows.

firefox: set the content blocks that a root pin cannot reach

Firefox 157 and 158 declare some colour names on the host of a
component, so a declaration of the root never reaches them. The content
sheet gains one scoped rule for each of these surfaces.

The category list of the settings takes the view hover. A menu row
takes the menu item hover. A row of a select list takes the highlight
pair under the pointer and under a press, as its selected option does.
A pressed colour input takes the press shade of the buttons. A vibrant
promo takes the field pair, and the card of the AI controls takes the
view hover.

The private page paints the dark half alone. Its canvas takes the
window colour of that half, and its search box takes the field colours
of that half. Its mask takes the highlight. The lists widget of the new
tab takes the page text and the highlight. The onboarding screens take
the page text and the link colours of their own half, and their primary
button takes the highlight ladder. The selected tab of a dialog takes
the link of its half in place of the primary accent, through a new role
of the map.

A block table holds the selector, the rows, the prefix and the value
form of each rule. The map lists the block rows under their prefixes.

firefox: keep the own colours of the blocked site page

The blocked site page paints white text on a red ground, with dark red
hover and press grounds. Its primary button paints black text on white,
and the links of its white description box are black. The names of the
accent block give it the text, the link and the button shades of the
scheme in place of them. Those read under 4.5:1 on the red ground. The
pressed link of the box reads under 4.5:1 on white. The highlight text
of a light scheme reads 1.0:1 on the white primary button. The content
sheet now ends with the rules for that page alone. They state the own
colours of the page under the same contrast condition as the page. The
links of the red canvas take the white text, and the links of the
description box keep their black.

firefox: pin the names of round D and check them in the build

The pin gains the 28 names that the content sheet now sets. Its header
names the host files that declare them. The build holds the floor of
the content runs of the name check from mkfloors.sh: 46 for the content
sheets. The chrome floor stays at 81, and the floor of the chrome rows
of the map stays at 25.

firefox: check the viewer rules, the page blocks and the pressed rows

The content sheet gains six page blocks after its accent block and one
root rule for the PDF viewer document with its descendant rules. Its
root rule gains two toolbar button names and the destructive button
text. The chrome root gains the plain button, the address bar box and
the app menu row names, and the plain button text. The notification
bar rule gains the border of the bar. The checks of the build follow.

The content check reads 29 names in the root rule of the accent block
in place of 26. The destructive text holds the pair of the browser:
white in the light half and the Nova grey 100 in the dark one. The
sheet holds eight scope lines. The viewer rule holds the focus colour
of the chrome sheet and its border colour in the half of the scheme.
Twenty-one control copies bite.

The block check of the content sheet reads the six page blocks in the
order of the writer. Each block gives its scope line, its rules and its
view rule, which holds the view text and the view ground at the field
pairs. In the viewer rule, the focus colour holds one hex colour. Each
other hex name and the mix hold their root values, each pair name its
root pair and each menu name the menu value. The thirteen descendant
rules follow, each name at the value of its anchor, and the editor bar
rule closes with its scheme line. Twenty-seven control copies bite.

The map check reads the rows of the page blocks and of the viewer
rules against the content sheets of both colour files. The chrome block
check reads the border of the notification bar at the root highlight.
The checks fail the writer of today, because its sheets hold none of
these rules. The writer changes follow in their own commits.

firefox: fill a pressed button from step 4 of the button ladder

The Qt style fills a sunken button from step 4 of the button ladder.
The writer painted the pressed ground of a button from step 2, the
step of the GTK sheets. Every pressed ground of the chrome sheet, the
content sheet and the manifest now takes step 4. The other content
half takes step 4 of the ladder of the box of the browser.

The pressed accent keeps step 2 of the highlight ladder, so a pressed
primary button and a pressed switch that is on do not move. The
manifest key of the pressed button follows the writer in both halves,
so the add-on changes with it.

firefox: pin the pressed and hovered grounds of the chrome

Under Nova, four chrome surfaces take violet or currentColor mixes
under the pointer and under a press. They are the cancel button of a
window-modal dialog, the bookmark star, the site information box and
the rows of the app menu. The chrome root rule gains the plain button
names and the address bar box names. They join the hover row and the
active row of the map. The plain button text joins the button text
row. The cancel button and the notification bar buttons read it from
the token file.

The root rule also gains the app menu row names in two new rows. They
hold the ground of a popup menu item under the pointer and its text.
The map holds 27 chrome rows.

firefox: pin the toolbar buttons and the destructive text of the pages

The toolbar type of a button in a page and the PDF viewer read the
toolbar button names of the chrome. The content root rule gains those
two names in the hover row and the active row of the pairs. A pressed
toolbar button in a page then takes step 4 of the button ladder.

The destructive text of the browser is white in the light half and the
Nova grey 100 in the dark one. The Nova layer of the token file routes
its dark half to the primary text. The pin of that text under a light
scheme read 1.3:1 on the red ground. The root rule now states the pair
of the browser, so the red ground keeps its own text.

firefox: give the view pages the view text and the view grounds

The tables and the lists of the about: pages took the page text and
the Nova grounds. A tree view or a table view of a QtCurve window
paints its rows in the view text on the view ground. It paints the
alternate rows on the alternate view ground and a hovered row on the
view hover.

The content sheet gains six page blocks after its accent block, one
per container family of the pages. A block holds the addresses of its
pages, the view grounds at its root and the view text on its view
container. The pages that inherit the root colour take a plain view
rule on their containers: the view text and the view ground. The table
of about:policies has no ground name of its own, and the box name
grounds the list of about:downloads.

The codec table header of about:support takes the window ground under
the view text. The page about:firefoxview takes its own root token. A
root pin of the page text inside a page block reaches the other text of
a page as well, so no block holds one.

A new role gives the alternate view ground of the scheme. The other
content half takes the box of the browser there, as the view ground
does. A page block table holds the scope, the rules and the selector
of the view rule of each block. The map lists the rows of each rule
under its prefix.

firefox: pin the roles of the PDF viewer at its root

The viewer document of pdf.js reads the colour tokens of Firefox. They
paint its toolbar, its sidebar, its dialogs, its menus and its buttons.
The accent block of the content sheet does not reach it. The address
of a PDF is the address of the file, so no address scope matches it.
The root of the viewer document alone carries the attribute that
blocks the selection print. The content sheet now holds one rule on
that attribute after the page blocks.

The rule gives the toolbar, the dialogs and the information box the
window colours. The sidebar, the fields and the menus take the view
ground. The buttons take the button ladder with the button text. The
toggled rows and the toggled buttons take step 4 of the button ladder.

The borders, the separators and the page area behind the pages take the
frame shade. The menu rows take the ground and the text of a popup menu
item under the pointer. The accent names, the links, the information
colours and the deemphasized mix restate their values of the accent
block. The focus colour takes the value of the chrome sheet. The map
lists the rows under one prefix.

firefox: pin the names that the PDF viewer declares on its elements

The viewer sheet declares its own colour names on twelve kinds of
elements. They are the tree views, the attachment rows, the views
manager, the dialogs, the signature tabs and the switches. They are
also the popup menus, the secondary toolbar, the dividers, the
thickness picker, the floating editor bar and the undo bar. A rule of
the viewer root cannot reach them.

The content sheet gains one descendant rule of the viewer root for
each of these elements. The tree rows and the attachment rows take the
view text, the view hover and the selection pair. The view buttons
take the button text. The fields of a dialog take the view ground and
the view text, and the lines of the signature tabs the frame shade.
The selected signature tab takes the window text. The dot of a switch
that is on takes the selection text.

A hovered row of the secondary toolbar takes the ground of a popup menu
item under the pointer. The floating editor bars and the undo bar take
the window colour. The text layer of a page holds one colour scheme, so
the editor bar rule closes with a scheme line of both halves. A last
rule gives the secondary text of the dialogs and the disabled menu text
the deemphasized mix of the content halves. The map lists each rule
under its own prefix.

firefox: give the notification bar border the highlight

The border of a notification bar reads its own name. With the system
colours off in the chrome, that name takes a violet step in the light
half. It takes a Nova grey in the dark one. The notification bar rule
of the chrome sheet gains the name at the highlight. That is the
colour of the bar icon and of the bar button border. A message widget
of KDE paints its border in the colour of its icon.

firefox: pin the names of round E and check them in the build

The pin gains the 56 names that the chrome sheet and the content sheet
now set. Its header names the host files that declare them, the viewer
sheet of pdf.js and the page sheets among them. The build holds the
floors of the name check from mkfloors.sh: 91 for the chrome sheets
and 104 for the content sheets. The floor of the chrome rows of the
map moves from 25 to 27.

firefox: check the selection rule of the login list

The content sheet gains one selection rule in the block of the row
pages, after the view rule of that block. The block check of the
content sheet now reads that rule. Its head names the list item of
the login list by its type and its two classes.

Its body holds four lines in the order of the writer. They are the
text at the root highlight text, the ground at the root highlight,
the image and the radius. The image holds the gradient of the
selection appearance to the bottom, with hex stops from 0 to 100
percent, or none. The radius holds pixels with one decimal. Seven new
control copies bite:
- the rule cut out;
- the ground at the hover accent;
- the text at the highlight, the colour of its ground;
- the rule after the close of its block;
- the image as one hex colour;
- the radius line cut out;
- the image as a gradient to the right.

The check fails the writer of today, because its content sheet holds
no selection rule. The writer change follows in its own commit.

firefox: give the selected login row the selection pair

about:logins selects its first login at load. The row paints the
canvas of the browser with the page text, and its border alone takes
the highlight. An item view of a QtCurve window paints a selected row
in the highlight under the selection appearance. The row takes the
highlight text and round corners from the round key.

The block of the row pages gains one selection rule on the list item
of the login list. The rule gives the row the highlight text and the
highlight as a flat ground. The gradient of the selection appearance
stands over that ground, as the GTK sheets paint a selected row. A
flat appearance gives no image. The corners take the selection radius
of the GTK sheets. The border keeps the value of the browser, which
the root pin of the accent gives the highlight already.

The row sits three shadow trees deep, and a user rule matches inside
them. The rule names the list item by its type, so it leaves the host
of the row alone: the host carries the same classes. The blank row of
a new login takes the same classes, and the hover rule of the browser
leaves a selected row alone. A page block table holds the selector of
the selection rule beside the selector of the view rule.

firefox: give the plain text fields of the about: pages the field pair

The config search, the login filter, the login edit fields and the
fields of about:url-classifier and about:networking paint the browser
box. They read no custom property of their own. The textbox rule of
the in-content sheet gives them the box, the page border and the
inherited text. A line edit of a QtCurve window paints the view ground
with the view text inside a frame line.

Three page blocks of the content sheet gain one field rule each, after
the rules of the block. The rule names the fields of its pages. It
gives them the field text, the field ground and the frame as the pairs
of the two content halves. The filter of the login list sits in a
shadow tree, and a user rule reaches it. The view mode of a login shows
its inputs as read-only labels, so the rule takes the text inputs
without the read-only state. The password display stays read-only in
the edit mode, so a form test on an editable input reaches it there.

A page block table holds the selector of the field rule beside the two
other rule selectors. The block check of the content sheet reads each
field rule after the selection rule of its block. Its three lines hold
the anchors of the field pairs and the frame pair. Eight new control
copies bite. The content check reads the field text at the floor on
the field ground beside the box.

firefox: pin the input and result button names of Firefox 159

Firefox 159 renames the input ground and the input border of its text
fields. The chrome sheet pins the old ground name alone, so the name
field of the tab group editor loses the view ground on 159. Seven
chrome fields keep it through the system field colour alone. The old
names stay for Firefox 157 and 158.

The field row of the chrome root and the tab group block gain the new
ground name. The root rule of the about: pages gains the two input
ground names at the view ground and the two border names at the frame.
